\ `array-n` packs a literal number of stack values into an array. With a literal
\ count the AOT compiler folds it to a concrete-arity array construction instead
\ of collapsing the abstract stack to an opaque row region, so the pack and the
\ values around it stay modeled. Under runtime-image AOT the word body is
\ compiled, so these results come from the compiled path and match the
\ interpreter: a plain three-value pack, a zero-count empty pack, and a pack
\ over a preceding row region introduced by a runtime-depth `drop-at`, with the
\ values below the row surviving the pack.
1 2 3 3 array-n inspect print-line
"---" print-line
0 array-n #len .
"---" print-line
1 2 3 4 2 drop-at
10 20 30 3 array-n inspect print-line
. . .
